			<TR>
				<TD colspan=2>The Following is a Repost of a message from Cordath on Castersrealms Forums:<BR><BR>I started out writing a post that just answered your questions, but it left a ton out and didn't flow very well so I am rewriting it in a different format. First is the basics, which tell you the minimum you need to know. Details follow.<BR>THE BASICS<BR>1. As an enchanter you should have your PB AE stuns up. (i.e. Color Flux, Color Shift, Color Skew, Color Slant) Load 3 at the very least. I will discuss these spells more below. <BR>2. Pick a spot to pull to and plant yourself. The rest of your group will stay as close to you as possible so that your stuns can protect them. <BR>3. Your puller(s) will bring in a bunch of mobs and you will chaincast your stuns over and over to "stunlock" them. Your nukers can then rip the crowd to shreds while taking relatively little damage. Unless insanely lucky you will get some resists and casting interrupts and, as a result, some damage will leak through. The important thing is to remain calm and keep those stuns coming. If you stop stunning your group will most likely perish. <BR>4. Watch the pretty exp messages go flying by.<BR><BR><BR>IN DETAIL<BR><BR><BR>Group Composition:<BR>Any serious AE groups needs 4 things. You can AE in a limited fasion without them all, but you really do need all 4 to make a good go of it.<BR><BR>1. Damage Preventors: Enchanters are the only real option here. Depending on the relative level of the enchanter to the mobs being AE'd one will do. Two will make larger pulls a great deal safer. Past 2 you tend to run into diminishing returns for a single group. <BR>2. Damage Dealers: You need casters capable of casting PB AE's. This includes from best to worst: Wizards, Magicians, Druids, Clerics, and Necromancers. There are some pretty big gaps here that depend on level. A 56+ wizard is far ahead of even a level 60 mage. Necros are so far behind at all levels that you can't even really consider them an AE class. Clerics are much more effective at dealing PB AE damage, oddly enough. Bards can also PB AE. I am not sure precisely where they fit in, but they are well above necros and probably clerics too. This does depend heavily on the quality of bard though.<BR>3. Damage Reversor: i.e. a healer. While druids, shamans, and even paly's will do in a pinch if you know what you're doing, a cleric is very handy to have. AE groups are inherantly high risk. With a cleric you can push the envelope and make insane exp even with a couple wipeouts. Clerics can also serve as the:<BR>4. Puller: While you can bring in a few mobs at a time and have the enchanters keep them stunned while the puller(s) go out for more, a puller capable of bringing in 20 or more mobs in a single train makes things go a lot faster. Some of the best AE zones are full of mobs that harmtouch. This means you either need a high HP meatshield such as a warrior, or more preferably, a DA capable class. This includes clerics, paladins, bards, and necromancers. Level requirments vary here... Paladins do not get DA until level 55, and bards do not get it until 60. Bards do get group DA though, which can be very very very handy.<BR><BR><BR>Your stuns<BR>You have four stuns. They vary in casting time, mana cost, duration, and area of effect. Color Flux is your fastest stun with only a 1 second casting time. It is much easier to channel through 20 enraged mobs than even Color Shift or Color Slant, which have 1.5 second casting times. On the downside, it has a much smaller are of effect that your other 3 stuns and is unlikely to stun all the mobs you are fighting unless they are very closely packed. For this reason, I suggest you use the other 3 stuns for holding a stunlock. However, keep Color Flux loaded to help you recover when your stunlock breaks. <BR><BR>Things to tell your nukers<BR>Make sure your nukers know that they should:<BR>1. Never leave your side. If they start running around they will die. Even if they stay in stun range, if they are too far away mobs will sit outside of stun range but will be in meelee range and beating on your nuker.<BR>2. Hold their fire till all mobs being pulled are present. Incremental pulls can take a while, and if your nukers start going too early they will run OOM before the last mobs even arrive. Not only is this dangerous, it also greatly increases downtime. This also applies when your puller is bringing in large trains all at once. It can take a while for all mobs to run into stun range, especially if some are casters. <BR>3. Once they start nuking, don't stop. If they are getting hit they should ignore it. AE'ing is not for the faint of heart after all. =P<BR><BR>Things to tell your Healer<BR>1. Do not use Celestial Elixir, Celestial Healer, or Torpor during a battle. While these spells do have impressive mana/HP ratios they are just too slow. They are great for casting on the puller (Well, not torpor for the puller) or enchanter at the very beginning of the pull though, since they will continue to heal for a while. <BR>2. Remedy or Divine Light are the heals to use during the battle. They are fast casting, which is of paramount importance. You can also use them on the puller during a pull since these spells have very long range. <BR>3. Keep the chanter(s) alive at all costs. Once they go down the group is fubar'd. I'm sure they can guess that much though. =P<BR><BR>Reducing Interrupts<BR>First off, always try to face your enemies. Mobs hit you harder and interrupt you easier from behind. This will reduce your interrupts a little, but not nearly as much as the following: Mobs can interrupt you in two ways. First, by stunning or smacking you really hard or second, by moving you. The second way is by far the worst of the two. When you have 20 mobs beating you up they will push you back quite fast. You can move forward to compensate for this since EQ only compares your position when you start casting and when you stop to calculate interrupts. However, a far better solution is to find a nice corner and wedge your behind into it. If mobs beat you into the corner they *cannot* move you an inch. This makes you much harder to interrupt. As a direct result you will be able to handle larger pulls with greater safety. <BR><BR>Reducing Interrupts further when AE'ing mobs with Proximity Aggro<BR>Some mobs are far more likely to attack what's closest to them than anything else. These are refered to as mobs with "proximity aggro" or somethings "lazy aggro". There is probably more lingo for this on other servers I am not familiar with. If your nukers and other group members are between you and the mobs, they will effectively take the proximity aggro off you. It is far better for your nukers to take a few proximity hits than for you to risk an interrupt that will get everyone pounded on while you recover your stunlock. Conversely, the worst thing you can do against mobs of this type is to run out into the middle of the pack. Not only does this expose your back and make you moveable, this also greatly increases the number of mobs that will be beating on you because you will be closer to them than anyone else. If mobs get spread out this may be unavoidable, however, you can keep mobs from getting spread out by:<BR><BR>Packing Mobs with Spell Pushes<BR>While you will note that your higher level stuns have a large area of effect, you should remember than most classes PB AE's do not have quite that much range. As a result, you will want to ensure that mobs are relatively close together so your nukers can hit them all. To do this, you need to take advantage of spell pushes. Go stunlock some newbie trash for an extended period of time. If your lock never falls you will notice that you are slowly pushing that mob in the direction you are facing. (Note that you are not pushing the mobs backwards from the way they are facing, but instead in the direction you are facing) Other classes AE nukes also do this, but to a much larger extent. (e.g. Wizards Jyll's line of spells are great pushers) If you are fighting in a corner, simply have your nukers face into the corner. (This will make some people nervous, but it really does work better) Every nuke will pull mobs into the corner and pack them tighter and tighter. If they faced out from the corner they would push mobs away from you instead, and force you to move out to keep them in stun range. You can still face out from the corner to reduce interrupts. The nukers will overcome your push easily. Just one wizard can easily outpush an enchanter. However, if your group is collecting mobs a few at a time you may want to start out facing the corner so that mobs don't get too spread out before your nukers start.<BR><BR>What to do when somebody dies<BR>If you lose your enchanter, try to evac or camp the cleric. Death will be likely. If you lose your healer you can either evac or pray your stunlock holds well enough to finish off the pull without heals. This is often survivable, but then you have to worry about getting the cleric back to the group. If you lose your puller just drag him in and rez him. If you lose a nuker, evac if your remaining nukers are going to go OOM before the mobs die. If you lose all your nukers it's time to camp the cleric or gate. Nothing too hard to figure out here. Basically, when something goes wrong in an AE group a wipeout is very likely. If you're pushing the envelope hard enough to get a couple wipeouts a night you should be making enough exp to more than make up for it however. <BR><BR>Some good AE zones<BR>-Howling Stones. North Wing is great for groups from low to mid fifties. Low HP mobs. Many do HT though so bring a good meatshield or DA puller. In the high 50's you will need to switch to the South or East wings. South is a bit tricky to pull. 2 DA pullers or a bard makes it very doable. East has some fun pulls, but you need more nukers than you do in the North or South. West doesn't pull that well and is hardly any tougher than the North so not really worth doing. <BR>-Sebilis: This place depends on your server. On some servers it's deserted and there is tons to pull. On others it's packed on a daily basis and you just can't get enough spawns to keep busy. <BR>-Karnors: If you go during off-peak hours you can AE pretty much the whole zone. During peak hours your best bet is the basement. <BR>-Velketors: Good off hours spot. Packed during primetime all too often.<BR>-Acrylia: Lots of low HP mobs. Tons of HT'ers though, so bring a DA puller. <BR><BR><BR>Glossary:<BR><BR>Stunlock: Chain casting stuns so that mobs are perpetually sunned<BR>PB AE: Person Based Area Effect spell. This is a nuke that is based around the caster and can hit an unlimited numbers of mobs. This makes it the weapon of choise for AE groups.<BR>Proximity Aggro: a.k.a "Lazy Aggro" Mobs that have this kind of aggro prefer to attack what's closest to them over almost anything else.<BR><BR><BR>Corvan -- Level 60 enchanter<BR>Cordath -- Level 60 wizard<BR><BR><BR>P.S. <a href=http://members.shaw.ca/testuart/46mobs.jpg>Here is why the Grey was Nerfed. </A><BR>
				</TD>
